[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Figure 1: Overview of the Cell2Text framework. The model takes single-cell RNA-seq profiles as input and processes them through a pretrained Geneformer encoder to generate contextualized gene-level embeddings. These embeddings are projected into the semantic space of the language model via a lightweight adapter module, aligning biological signals with linguistic representations. A pretrained, instruction-tuned LLM decoder then generates structured natural language descriptions that capture cellular identity, tissue of origin, disease associations, and pathway activity.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic workflow for a cell-description generation or multimodal language-model training setup.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Input biological source object: a single-cell gene expression vector labeled “cell 1: Gene Z Gene E ... Gene C”.\n- Cell metadata block lists attributes such as cell id, cell type “vein endothelial cell”, development stage “prime adult stage”, disease “normal”, sex “male”, tissue “caudate lobe of liver”, tissue general “liver”, and pathway labels including “HALLMARK_COAGULATION” and “HALLMARK_MYC_TARGETS_V2”.\n- A textual target/output caption labeled “cell description (Cp)” states that the sample consists of a vein endothelial cell and an endothelial cell that is part of a vein.\n- Transformations:\n  - Gene expression is passed through a “cell encoder”.\n  - Output becomes “gene embeddings”.\n  - Gene embeddings are passed through a “modality adapter”.\n  - The adapter maps embeddings into dimensions labeled 1152, 2048, and 3072.\n- Model interface:\n  - A sequence of embeddings is fed into a “language model decoder”.\n  - Embedding types are color-coded and labeled:\n    - green: instruction embeddings\n    - orange: gene embeddings\n    - green: prompt embeddings\n    - gray: generated/next-token-related embeddings\n- Training objective:\n  - The decoder predicts “next token probability p”.\n  - Loss is shown as `L_sft = L_CrossEntropy(Cp, p)`.\n- Finding/claim shown by the figure:\n  - The diagram illustrates supervised fine-tuning of a language model decoder to generate cell descriptions from gene-expression-derived embeddings plus instruction and prompt embeddings."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 16, "caption": "Figure 2: Token length distribution of gene expression sequences after tokenization with the Geneformer tokenizer.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThis is a single-panel quantitative plot: a histogram titled “Length Distribution - combined.”\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis label: “Length”\n- Y-axis label: “Frequency”\n- Light blue histogram bars showing a length distribution.\n- Red dashed vertical reference line labeled in the legend as “Mean: 1843.2.”\n- The distribution spans roughly from low hundreds to just over 4000 length units.\n- There is a prominent high-frequency spike near the far right around length ~4000.\n\nBiological/source objects:\n- No visible biological specimens, microscopy images, cells, tissues, organisms, molecules, or sequence logos are shown.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- No model architecture, image transformation pipeline, segmentation overlay, embedding plot, or interface diagram is visible.\n\nFinding shown:\n- The figure summarizes the combined frequency distribution of lengths, with a reported mean of 1843.2 and a large concentration at the maximum/right-end length range."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 16, "caption": "Figure 3: Token length distribution of natural language descriptions after tokenization with the Llama-3.21B-Instruct tokenizer.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single-panel histogram showing the distribution of tokenized `natural_desc` lengths for a combined dataset. The x-axis is labeled `# tokens`, and the y-axis is labeled `Frequency`. The title reads `Tokenized natural_desc Length - combined`.\n\nThe histogram uses light green bars, with most observations concentrated roughly between 70 and 130 tokens and a right-skewed tail extending beyond 300 tokens. A red dashed vertical line marks the mean, with the legend indicating `Mean: 104.3`.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, model architecture diagrams, or transformation pipelines are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 17, "caption": "Figure 4: Overview of the distribution of cell types in the dataset. For clarity, only the 30 most abundant categories out of 783 are shown.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel vertical bar chart showing counts by biological cell-type categories.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis label: `Categories`\n- Y-axis label: `Count`\n- Bars are sorted descending by count and colored with a blue-to-red gradient.\n- Numeric count labels are shown above each bar.\n- The highest categories include:\n  - `oligodendrocyte`: 29,725\n  - `neuron`: 27,981\n  - `CA4 pyramidal alpha neuron`: 21,994\n  - `T cell`: 20,957\n  - `fibroblast`: 20,220\n  - `glutamatergic neuron`: 19,635\n  - `CD8-positive, alpha-beta T cell`: 18,755\n  - `B cell`: 18,206\n- Lower-count categories visible include immune, neuronal, stromal, and tissue-associated cell types such as `microglial cell`, `plasma cell`, `neutrophil`, and `stromal cell`.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Cell-type categories, including neural cells, immune cells, epithelial cells, fibroblasts, macrophages, monocytes, and stromal cells.\n\nTransformations / model interfaces:\n- The figure appears to summarize categorical frequency counts, likely from a biological dataset or cell annotation output.\n- No model architecture, embedding, microscopy image, or algorithmic interface is shown.\n\nFinding:\n- The distribution is imbalanced, with oligodendrocytes and neurons having the largest counts, while categories such as neutrophil and stromal cell have the smallest visible counts."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "p